":1691007959833,"updateDate":1691007959833}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959833, 1691007959833),
    ($SATQ$939c46d1$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$070902-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p><img src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/939c46d1/img_000_7d3edfed.png" alt="The figure presents a cylindrical shape with a circular base and a larger circular top. The diameter of the circular base is labeled &ldquo;k over 2,&rdquo; the diameter of the circular top is labeled &ldquo;k,&rdquo; and the height is labeled &ldquo;k.&rdquo; The volume of the figure is equal to the fraction with numerator 7 pi k cubed, and denominator 48" width="153" height="119">
          <p class="stem_paragraph ">The glass pictured above can hold a maximum volume of 473 cubic centimeters, which is approximately 16 fluid ounces. Jenny has a pitcher that contains 1&nbsp;gallon of water. How many times could Jenny completely fill the glass with 1&nbsp;gallon of water? <span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/939c46d1/img_001_60a60ceb.png" alt="1 gallon equals 128 fluid ounces"></span></span></p></p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p>16</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p>&nbsp; 8</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p>&nbsp; 4</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p>&nbsp; 3</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is correct. It is given that the volume of the glass is approximately 16 fluid ounces. If Jenny has 1 gallon of water, which is 128 fluid ounces, she could fill the glass <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/939c46d1/img_002_1eb71a66.png" alt="128 over 16, which equals 8"></span> times.<p>Choice A is incorrect because Jenny would need <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/939c46d1/img_003_650d3aaa.png" alt="16 times 16"></span> fluid ounces = 256 fluid ounces, or 2 gallons, of water to fill the glass 16 times. Choice C is incorrect because Jenny would need only <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/939c46d1/img_004_a1997d75.png" alt="4 times 16"></span> fluid ounces = 64 fluid ounces of water to fill the glass 4 times. Choice D is incorrect because Jenny would need only <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/939c46d1/img_005_8aed7faa.png" alt="3 times 16"></span> fluid ounces = 48 fluid ounces to fill the glass 3 times.</p></p>
